WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.768 size:78% position:39%
-Welcome to the show.
-Great to be here.

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:03.003 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think most people
who know you

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:03.803 line:92%
know you from election night

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:06.306 align:start size:94% position:6%
You are a superstar
on election night.

00:00:06.306 --> 00:00:09.542 align:start size:94% position:6%
You work the map.
You are often very frazzled.

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:11.745 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we actually cut together
a highlight reel.

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:16.182
This is from a special election.
in Pennsylvania, 2018.

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:17.083 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Let's take a look.

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:17.951 line:92%
-Kasie, and I have to stop you

00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:20.620 align:start size:94% position:6%
Steve Kornacki has a piece o
paper in his mouth.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.422 align:start size:94% position:6%
Steve, I keep wondering
if you are

00:00:22.422 --> 00:00:23.857 line:92%
looking for my attention.

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:26.493 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you -- Okay. Pen, pen.
Okay.

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:30.163 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, I -- We're gonna just --
Let's go to math class.

00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:31.431 line:92%
-You've probably got to get --

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:34.701 align:start size:94% position:6%
Let's say you've got to
get 1,625.

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:35.502 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-1,625.

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:37.904 size:78% position:39%
-It's early morning.
You're awake with me.

00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:38.605 size:78% position:39% line:92%
How exciting is this?

00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:41.174 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've got an election
coming down to absentees

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:43.076 align:start size:94% position:6%
in Washington County,
Pennsylvania.

00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:45.111 line:92%
We all get to watch it together!

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:46.246 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Very enthusiastic.

00:00:46.246 --> 00:00:47.847 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's a joy to watch.
-Thank you.

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:49.883 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And, of course,
couldn't help notice,

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:51.051 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of the things that was
making that night

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:53.787
particularly hard -- you had
a cast on one of your hands.

00:00:53.787 --> 00:00:55.321 align:start size:94% position:6%
Was that --
That was a recent injury, yes?

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:57.557 align:start size:94% position:6%
-The day before that --
or two days before that --

00:00:57.557 --> 00:01:00.393 align:start size:94% position:6%
I had gone over
the handlebars on a bike.

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:02.429 align:start size:94% position:6%
So that was --
I had a broken hand.

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:03.563 line:92%
And I had to go to surgery about

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:05.565 size:81% position:41%
10:00 a.m. the morning
after that election.

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:06.966 line:92%
So, we were up until 5:00 a.m.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:08.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
waiting for the final vote
to come in.

00:01:08.535 --> 00:01:10.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
I had no sleep
and then I went to surgery

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:10.970 line:92%
as soon as I got out of there.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:14.474
-Well, you did a fantastic job
Way to play through the injury

00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:15.075 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Thank you.

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:20.013
-So, I'm really enjoying
your impeachment podcast.

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:21.347 size:78% position:39%
You both sort of fill
everybody in on --

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:24.517
You know, 'cause, again, there's
just, like, a new name,

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:25.285 size:78% position:39% line:92%
a new face every day.

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:27.353 align:start size:94% position:6%
You give us context as to
who these people are.

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:29.122 align:start size:94% position:6%
You also give
historical context.

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:31.157 align:start size:94% position:6%
How is this comparing
right now to the

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:32.759 align:start size:94% position:6%
Clinton impeachment
and the Nixon impeachment?

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:35.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, I mean, just if you loo
at the politics of it,

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:36.896 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think there's a stronger
comparison to

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:40.100
the Clinton impeachment 20 years
ago than to the Nixon one.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I say that because
of polarization.

00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:44.137 size:78% position:39%
It's that word we use
all the time, right?

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:45.305 line:92%
The party is being so dug in

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:48.241
When you look back at Watergate,
the Nixon impeachment drive,

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:49.976 align:start size:94% position:6%
what you saw was --
every new development,

00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:52.846 align:start size:94% position:6%
every new revelation,
public opinion moved with it

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:55.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, you know, support for
impeachment started very low

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:58.318 align:start size:94% position:6%
It started very low
in both parties in 1973.

00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:01.121 size:78% position:39%
By the summer of '74,
when Nixon resigned,

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:02.122 line:92%
it was overwhelming support,

00:02:02.122 --> 00:02:03.656 align:start size:94% position:6%
about 2/3 majority
in the polling.

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.859
Even among Republicans, ther
was support for impeachment.

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:08.194 size:78% position:39%
So, you look at that
and you compare it to

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:11.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
today, and, still, you know,
Democrats are almost

00:02:11.531 --> 00:02:12.565 line:92%
universally in favor of it

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:14.200 size:81% position:41%
Republicans are almost
universally opposed.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.403 align:start size:94% position:6%
You just see polarization righ
now in the numbers on this

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:19.272 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And, so, now, of course,
starting tomorrow,

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:22.208 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're going to see the
first of our public hearings

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:23.776 align:start size:94% position:6%
Democrats --
I should start by saying,

00:02:23.776 --> 00:02:25.979 size:78% position:39%
Republicans have been
clamoring for this

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:26.946 line:92%
to be brought into the public.

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:29.949 align:start size:94% position:6%
Democrats have allowed it to b
brought into the public.

00:02:29.949 --> 00:02:32.418 align:start size:94% position:6%
The idea of being that
maybe this will

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:33.286 size:75% position:38% line:92%
tip the scales more.

00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:34.954 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yet, with this polarizatio
you're talking about,

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:37.857 align:start size:94% position:6%
it strikes me as very unlikely
there will ever be --

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:39.192 line:92%
should this go to the Senate

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:40.493 line:92%
that there will ever be enough

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:42.795 align:start size:94% position:6%
Republican senators
willing to switch sides

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:43.730 line:92%
to impeach the president.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.198 line:92%
Is that your thinking as of now?

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:46.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I mean,
politicians follow the polls

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:48.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
One of the reasons we're alway
putting the polls up there

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:51.304 align:start size:94% position:6%
is because they guide
the actions of politicians

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:53.940 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if you're a Republican
politician right now

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.109 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're trying to make sens
of what's going on

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:58.945
in your own party --
Remember, if you lose a primary,

00:02:58.945 --> 00:02:59.579 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
you lose your job.

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:02.982
And I think they see their party
almost universally, as I said,

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:06.886
behind Trump, almost universally
against impeachment.

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:08.555 align:start size:94% position:6%
You think back to the
2016 campaign.

00:03:08.555 --> 00:03:11.357 size:78% position:39%
Donald Trump won that
nomination in 2016,

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.126 align:start size:94% position:6%
basically going around
all of these

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:13.960 line:92%
Republican elected officials

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.229 align:start size:94% position:6%
He didn't have a single
endorsement from a Republica

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:19.232 align:start size:94% position:6%
in Congress until he won the
New Hampshire primary,

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:22.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I think that delivered
a very powerful

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:23.636 line:92%
psychological message, really,

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:25.705 align:start size:94% position:6%
to Republican members of
Congress that,

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:26.940 size:81% position:41% line:92%
"Wow," they would say.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:28.107 size:88% position:44% line:92%
"Our voters, our people,

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:30.843 align:start size:94% position:6%
the people who elect me,
are with him.

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:31.544 size:75% position:38% line:92%
I'd better be, too."

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:33.379 align:start size:94% position:6%
-There -- Isn't there also
an argument --

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:34.914 size:78% position:39%
Let's say it never is
going to lead to him

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:38.751
getting removed from office,
but bringing these public,

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:41.554 align:start size:94% position:6%
you will then have to
force politicians

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:46.292 align:start size:94% position:6%
to choose a side and be on
the record for history

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:49.095
as where they landed, especially
as more and more comes out

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:50.697 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the things that
they said a month ago --

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:52.398 size:81% position:41%
"Look, if it was this,
I'd feel differently."

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:53.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then this happens,
and they're like,

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:55.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
"No, and now
it's got to be that."

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:56.669 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that happens, and
they're, like,

00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:58.037 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Guys, I don't know
what to tell you."

00:03:58.037 --> 00:03:59.739 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, isn't there
some value in that?

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:00.306 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Well, yeah.

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:01.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think there is a political
angle on it, too,

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:04.344 align:start size:94% position:6%
just in terms of, sure,
there's a lot of Republicans -

00:04:04.344 --> 00:04:05.778 align:start size:94% position:6%
Lindsey Graham,
I think you might be

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:07.046 line:92%
referring to right there.

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:07.480 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Sure.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.382
-Lindsey Graham's got to run
for re-election next year.

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:10.016 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:10.016 --> 00:04:12.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
-His big challenge in
South Carolina is

00:04:12.352 --> 00:04:13.253 size:84% position:42% line:92%
the Republican primary.

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:15.622 align:start size:94% position:6%
But there are other
Republican Senators --

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:16.889 size:84% position:42% line:92%
Susan Collins in Maine.

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:17.690 line:92%
Maine's a blue state, right?

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
Maine's a state that went
for Hillary Clinton.

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:21.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
Cory Gardner in Colorado -
blue state.

00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:23.630 align:start size:94% position:6%
They've got to balance
the demands

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:24.897 line:92%
of the Republican primary with

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:26.733 size:81% position:41%
"Hey, I got to win a
general election, too,

00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:27.433 line:92%
and that could be close."

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:28.468 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think those are
the ones --

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:31.104
if this does get to the Senate
if this does become a trial,

00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:33.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
they've got the trickiest
political calculation to make,

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:34.907 align:start size:94% position:6%
because, on the one hand,
they don't want to

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:36.943
anger that Republican base
get a primary challenge.

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:40.079
On the other hand, they coul
anger general-election voter

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:41.648 size:78% position:39% line:92%
who don't like Trump.

